    "explanation": "To the rider and to the driver. WHAT IS AGREED. The recommended route was 10.00 km and the trip as driven was 11.40 km (EV-TRIP-05003). The deviation is therefore 1.40 km (POL-RD-22), above the 1.00 km tolerance, and neither side disputes it; the driver-device trace (EV-GPS-05003) shows the same movement, the vehicle leaving the CTE at Bendemeer Road at 19:00:36 and rejoining at Upper Serangoon Road/Braddell Road at 19:11:06. WAS THE REROUTE TRAFFIC-JUSTIFIED? Yes (POL-RD-23). The LTA traffic advisory in the record (EV-ADVIS-05003-01, weight 1.0, complete) reports an accident on the Central Expressway (CTE) towards SLE, after the Balestier Road exit, valid 18:37:44 to 19:49:44, which covers this trip's 18:49:44-19:12:44 window. The recommended route in the trip record runs along the CTE towards SLE (optimal_route_via), and the platform's own deviation event records that the recommended route at the point the driver left it was the CTE. The accident therefore lies on that CTE stretch, in the direction of travel, inside the trip window, and the advisory intersects the recommended route. The rider is right that the platform's derived reading left this 'undetermined' and the record does not decide it for the parties, but POL-RD-23 asks the decision to be made from the advisory's road, direction and location against the trip, and on those it does intersect. I do not find this a fact the record cannot establish, and I decline to escalate on the rider's POL-GEN-26 ground, and equally on the driver's alternative request to escalate on the same point. WAS THE RIDER INFORMED AND DID THE RIDER AGREE? Yes (POL-RD-21). The driver's in-app message MSG-05003-003 ('CTE got accident after Balestier, very jam. I exit Bendemeer and go Upper Serangoon ok? Should be faster.', EV-CHAT-05003) was sent 18:57:46 and read 18:58:03, about three minutes before the 19:00:36 deviation. The rider replied MSG-05003-004 ('Ok sure, thanks for telling') at 18:58:26, read 18:58:49. The rider argues this is a bare acknowledgment, not agreement to 1.40 km of extra metered distance. POL-RD-21 answers it: where the chat log confirms the rider's acknowledgment, the deviation is agreed. The log confirms it, so the deviation was agreed, and the case does not fall outside POL-RD-24's rows on that ground. WHICH ROW (POL-RD-24). Exactly one row applies. Traffic-justified; rider informed and agreed; deviation over 1.00 km: row (a), no refund. The rider's claim for 4.50 SGD is not supported. For completeness, the refund rows would have capped recovery at the excess distance fare: 1.40 km x the 0.72 SGD per km rate = 1.01 SGD, without surge, far below the 4.50 SGD claimed and against a 22.60 SGD fare; the rider's own advocate conceded that figure. The driver's point that no row could award 4.50 SGD is correct but does not arise. The rider's further point that the reroute was not actually faster (23.0 minutes against the recommended 20.2 minutes) is accurate but immaterial: POL-RD-23 justifies a reroute on the traffic data, not on whether it saved time. DECISION (POL-GEN-03). The rider filed, so the ruling is read against the rider's claim. That claim is not supported and the present state stands: NO_ACTION, no refund, no driver compensation, and no money moves - the 22.60 SGD fare already charged stands. WHO DECIDES (POL-RD-25, POL-GEN-22). The contested value is 4.50 SGD, below the 10.00 SGD band; the driver's profile carries fraud_flags 0, so the 0.95 uplift does not bite; the route-deviation bar is 0.80 and this ruling is issued at 0.85. The AI's 2 km deviation limit is not applied (thresholds.ai_ruling_max_deviation_applied is false), and 1.40 km would clear it in any event. No safety flag applies, and a route deviation carries no referral clause, so there is no referral."
